# Copy to dev/dev.env and edit. Loaded by both dev.ps1 and dev.sh on every run.
# dev.env is gitignored, so your local paths stay out of the repository.
#
# Format is KEY=VALUE. No quotes, no export, no variable expansion - values are
# taken verbatim, so a Windows path with backslashes and spaces is fine as-is.

# Where the database, logs and the whole OCR runtime live.
# Defaults to a sibling of the repository, which keeps the multi-GB model weights
# on the same drive as your checkout instead of on C:.
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_DATA_DIR=D:\ocr-data

# Port for the API. Must be 1024-65535, default 8084.
# Changing it also means changing BACKEND_PORT in apps/web/vite.config.ts, since
# the web dev server proxies /api there.
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_PORT=8084

# Path to the uv binary that builds the Python runtime.
# Defaults to <repo>/vendor/uv-<arch>/uv.exe (or .../uv). vendor/ is gitignored and
# fetched at build time, so set this if yours lives elsewhere.
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_UV_BINARY=C:\tools\uv\uv.exe

# --- Licensing -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The Speedbits License Manager. Leave these unset and registration/activation
# simply fail with "the licence server could not be reached"; nothing else in the
# app depends on them.
#
# These are INSTALLER API KEYS, not licence keys, and the difference matters:
#
#   installer API key  IMC_xxxx   identifies the BUILD. One per product, the same for
#                                 every customer, compiled into the app. Yours.
#   licence key        IMC-1234   identifies the CUSTOMER. One per user, emailed to
#                                 them, typed into the app. Theirs.
#
# Both are sent on every activation call. The one below is the first. It is therefore NOT a secret in this product:
# the source is published, so anything compiled into it is public. Treat it as a
# build tag that the licence server can revoke, and rotate it per release. It is
# kept out of the repository anyway, because a key committed to a public repo is
# indexed by scrapers within minutes and would be burned before the release ships.
# Two products, each with its own short_code AND its own installer key. The licence
# server rejects a key used against the other product with INVALID_API_KEY, which
# surfaces to the user as a broken licence rather than as a wrong build flag.
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_PRODUCT_COMMUNITY=impressiveocrcommunity
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_INSTALLER_KEY_COMMUNITY=IMC_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_PRODUCT_COMMERCIAL=impressiveocrcommercial
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_INSTALLER_KEY_COMMERCIAL=IMP_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Base URL of the licence server. Defaults to https://license.speedbits.io.
# The API documentation gives it as http; https is used deliberately, because every
# call carries an email address and a licence key. Only set this to override.
#IMPRESSIVE_OCR_LICENSE_URL=https://license.speedbits.io
